// Design prototype for GitHub issue #344: a slot pool with NO compile-time
// ceiling on the number of live objects at all -- not even a hidden one.
//
// growable_pool -- now linux_user/growable_pool/growable_pool_lib.tkb,
// off the product surface since kernel stacks outgrew a one-page chunk --
// already made the PAYLOAD storage lazy (one physical page per chunk,
// pulled on demand), but its own identity
// bookkeeping stayed a pair of `[usize; N]` arrays (FreelistCore(N)'s
// next_free plus its own occupant_generation), so a caller still had to
// pick N -- and MAX_CHUNKS, and SLOTS_PER_CHUNK, and keep
// `N == SLOTS_PER_CHUNK * MAX_CHUNKS` true by hand, with nothing checking
// it. That is the "guess a ceiling ahead of time" question issue #26 set
// out to remove; growable_pool only moved it one layer down. Issue #344
// asks for the layer to go away.
//
// Current limitations (2026-08-17). Keep this list current as call sites
// change; a limitation that goes stale here is worse than none, since it
// invites relying on a guarantee that quietly stopped holding.
// - A T CONTAINING STABLE OWNER STORAGE must be allocated through
//   intrusive_pool_insert_zeroed, not intrusive_pool_insert. The
//   language's empty state for such a field is "declaration-order tag
//   zero", inherited from a zero-initialized global rather than written
//   by anyone, and a recycled slot is neither zero nor fresh -- the
//   second use of one otherwise hands back a linear value no allocation
//   produced (issue #369, demonstrated). This is CHECKED, not merely
//   documented: intrusive_pool_insert carries
//   `static_assert(contains_stable_owner(T) == 0, ...)`, so allocating
//   such a T through it is a compile error naming the entry point to use
//   instead. Types that hold no linear handles pay nothing and keep
//   issue #356's contract exactly.
// - A CONSUMER MUST SUPPLY A LOCK BACKEND. This file names
//   pool_lock_acquire/pool_lock_release without saying who provides them
//   (issue #364), so a kernel consumer must have kernel/lib/pool_lock.tkb
//   on its file list and linux_user has its own checking stand-in.
//   Forgetting is an undefined-function error at the first `use`, not a
//   silent fallback -- but it is the one thing adopting this file costs
//   beyond `use`ing it.
// - Mutation requires this pool's guard, and that is enforced rather than
//   asked for (issue #351): insert, remove and reclaim take `borrow
//   IntrusivePoolGuard[pool_id]`, obtainable only from
//   intrusive_pool_lock on the same pool. What the guard does NOT do is
//   exclude: intrusive_pool_lock delegates to pool_lock_acquire, which
//   the file list supplies (issue #364), and linux_user's supplies
//   checking rather than exclusion. The port supplies disable_irq on one
//   core or a real acquire for SMP; nothing in this file changes when it
//   does.
// - READS are not guarded. A caller that reads while another context
//   mutates -- a cursor walk racing an insert, a counter racing a
//   reclaim -- must hold the guard across both, and this file cannot
//   make it. Guarding the observers too was tried and rejected: it costs
//   every caller a release on every early-return path, for a race the
//   mutation guard already makes impossible to reach WITHOUT a
//   deliberate concurrent reader. Revisit when there is one.
// - The guard is not re-entrant, and taking one twice on a path would
//   deadlock under a real lock. It is not a compile error -- both
//   acquires are individually correct, so nothing in the type ties the
//   second to the first -- but linux_user's lock body counts it, along
//   with two pools acquired in inconsistent orders at different sites,
//   which is the deadlock that only appears under the interleaving that
//   hits it (issue #365, linux_user/intrusive_pool/pool_lock_check.tkb).
//   A kernel build supplies a different body and pays none of that, so
//   the checking is thorough exactly where it is free.
// - Reclaim holds back up to `retained_chunks` fully-empty chunks per
//   pool and returns every later one, so steady-state memory is
//   `ceil(live / slots_per_chunk)` pages plus that reserve. It defaults
//   to 1 -- the anti-thrash choice issue #346 argued for -- and is set
//   per pool at init since issue #354, because how much churn a pool sees
//   is a property of what it holds. `intrusive_pool_reclaim` gives the
//   reserve back on demand; there is no global sweep, and that entry
//   point's comment says why not.
// - Slot addresses are NOT stable across reclaim. A cursor walk that
//   frees as it goes can have the chunk under it released; the exerciser
//   restarts its walk after every free for exactly this reason. What
//   stays sound regardless is validation: the moment a chunk is
//   unlinked, intrusive_pool_slot_is_valid rejects every address in it,
//   and if the same page comes back later as a chunk of this same pool,
//   the pool-global (never per-chunk) generation counter is what still
//   rejects a stale handle.
// - A chunk quarantined by the alloc-path check (issue #352) is leaked on
//   purpose: its page is never returned to the provider and it is never
//   allocated from again. That is bounded by the number of corruption
//   events, which is zero on a correctly behaving system -- but it does
//   mean a pool under a repeated stray write bleeds a page per event
//   rather than failing loudly. Revisit if that ever stops being the
//   right trade.
// - Validation derives its bounds and stores none, so the chunk header
//   cannot weaken it (issue #361). What the header IS still trusted for
//   is the free chain itself: `free_head` and the links. A corrupted
//   `free_head` is caught on the alloc path (issue #352); a corrupted
//   chunk or partial link is not, and would misdirect iteration or
//   reclaim rather than a dereference. Defending those needs something
//   the derivation trick cannot give, since unlike the bounds they are
//   genuinely dynamic -- a header checksum, or the invariant probe issue
//   #355 asks for.
// - `intrusive_pool_ref` does NOT re-validate. It is the fast path, with
//   the same contract kernel/lib/freelist.tkb's freelist_ref has: the
//   `borrow IntrusiveOwner[pool_id, _]` parameter proves, through
//   linearity and the brand, that the caller holds a live allocation from
//   THIS pool. A caller that round-trips a handle through
//   intrusive_owner_into_parts (the only way to fabricate one) must
//   re-validate with intrusive_pool_matches first. The SCANNING path has
//   no such caveat left: intrusive_pool_probe_slot validates and reads
//   the generation itself, and its Live view is the only key to the
//   payload.
// - A view is a SNAPSHOT. It says the slot was live when probed, which a
//   later insert or remove can undo -- which is why
//   intrusive_pool_payload_of sinks it rather than borrowing it, so one
//   probe cannot justify a second look. Holding the resulting `*T` across
//   a mutation is the caller's problem, exactly as it is for
//   intrusive_pool_ref's; what changed is that getting the pointer at all
//   now requires having looked.
// - T must be at least pointer-sized and must not need stronger than
//   8-byte alignment, both because the free-chain link shares its
//   storage and because `[u8; N]` contributes no alignment of its own
//   (issue #348). Both are static_asserts in
//   intrusive_pool_slots_per_chunk, measured rather than assumed. A T
//   that violates either does not compile; neither is a runtime failure
//   path, and neither can be silently misaligned.
// - A T whose SLOT exceeds one whole page cannot be pooled at all -- a
//   page-sized slot is now exactly poolable, since issue #349 took the
//   header out of the page. That is a compile-time property of T and is
//   enforced as one, alongside the check that the provider's per-page
//   metadata is large enough for a chunk header. No runtime failure path
//   exists for any of them.
// - The chunk header lives in storage the PAGE PROVIDER owns, so this
//   file's memory footprint is no longer self-contained: a pool costs its
//   80 static bytes plus its chunks. Since issue #350 a chunk carries its
//   own 128-byte header at its far end, so the cost is per CHUNK rather
//   than per page -- at order 0 that is what issue #349's per-page
//   reservation cost, and at order 3 it is the same 128 bytes spread over
//   eight pages.
//
// THE PAGE PROVIDER IS NOT NAMED HERE, DELIBERATELY (GitHub issue #364)
//
// This file `use`s no provider. It names the nine symbols it needs and
// binds to whichever provider is on the compiler's file list:
//
//   PAGE_SIZE
//   PageOwnerTag  PageRunAllocResult
//   page_alloc_contiguous  page_run_physical_address
//   page_run_owner_transfer_index  page_run_transferred_release_physical
//   page_owner_tag_at
//   pool_lock_acquire  pool_lock_release
//
// linux_user passes linux_user/growable_pool/fake_page_provider.tkb; a
// kernel build passes kernel/mm/page.tkb. One source, two providers, and
// nothing to keep in sync -- which is a claim the provider list above has
// to be kept honest for, since a provider missing one of these fails at
// link time rather than silently.
//
// The alternative -- copy the file on promotion, as growable_pool was --
// is what this avoids, and the evidence is that growable_pool's two
// copies now differ by 329 lines of code: its linux_user half is still on
// the pre-#314 `*T` style, still carries issue-#15 workarounds, and is
// missing a variant case, while passing its tests the whole time. A
// prototype that has quietly stopped describing what ships is worse than
// no prototype.
//
// ORDER MATTERS: the provider must appear BEFORE this file on the
// command line, because constants resolve as the parser walks the list
// left to right. Reversed, the build stops at the first array size or
// static_assert naming PAGE_SIZE -- loudly, and pointing at the
// constant, which is why this is a build-file convention rather than a
// hazard.
//
// The mechanism is the one Linux's SLUB and NetBSD's pool_cache/uma use:
// the free list is INTRUSIVE -- threaded through the free slots' own
// memory inside each chunk's page, one chain per chunk -- so there is no
// separate table to size. Concretely:
//
//   page provider's per-page metadata, indexed by page number
//   +----------------------------------------------------------+
//   | IntrusiveChunkHeader: chunk-list and partial-list links,  |
//   |   this chunk's own free_head, live_count, pool_tag        |
//   |   (GitHub #349; the slot bounds are derived, not stored --|
//   |    GitHub #361)                                           |
//   +----------------------------------------------------------+
//                            | describes
//                            v
//   chunk (2^order contiguous pages, aligned to its own size, from
//   page_alloc_contiguous() -- issue #350)
//   +----------------------------------------------------------+
//   | IntrusiveSlot(T) #0: generation, storage (payload OR link)|
//   | IntrusiveSlot(T) #1: generation, storage (payload OR link)|
//   | ...                     -- all 4096 bytes, no reservation |
//   +----------------------------------------------------------+
//
// and IntrusivePool(T) itself holds plain scalars and not one array: two
// list heads, the held-back empty chunk, a generation counter, and
// counters. Its whole static footprint is 80 bytes regardless of how many
// objects will ever live in it, and its runtime footprint is
// `ceil(live / slots_per_chunk)` pages plus the reserve it was told to
// hold (issue #354)
// -- in both directions, since an emptied chunk's page goes back to the
// provider (issue #346). The only remaining ceiling is the real, physical
// one -- page.tkb running out of pages -- which is what issue #344 asked
// for.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT A PARAMETER
//
// IntrusivePool(T) takes ONE generic argument: the payload type. There is
// no N, no MAX_CHUNKS, and (unlike growable_pool) no SLOTS_PER_CHUNK
// either: `sizeof(IntrusiveSlot(T))` is a compile-time constant AFTER
// monomorphization, so slot stride and slots-per-chunk are both derived
// inside this file (intrusive_pool_slot_stride /
// intrusive_pool_slots_per_chunk) rather than hand-computed and passed in
// by every caller. That removes growable_pool's whole class of
// caller-maintained arithmetic invariant -- there is no longer anything
// for a caller to get wrong, which is a stronger guarantee than checking
// it would have been.
//
// (growable_pool could not do this because `sizeof(T)` may not appear in
// an array-size or generic-argument position -- it does not need to here,
// because nothing in this design is sized by a generic argument.)
//
// WHAT THE HANDLE IS
//
// IntrusiveOwner[pool_id, allocation] carries the slot's ADDRESS plus its
// generation, rather than a slot index plus generation. An index would
// need an index -> address table, which is exactly the `N`-sized array
// this design exists to delete. The generation half is unchanged from the
// mechanism this codebase already standardized on (kernel/lib/
// slotmap.tkb's occupant_generation, issues #217/#296): a slot's
// generation word is stamped on allocate, zeroed on free, and a handle
// only matches while both agree.
//
// It is deliberately a DISTINCT linear type, not a reuse of
// kernel/lib/freelist.tkb's FreelistOwner (which growable_pool reuses).
// FreelistOwner's payload is a small array index; this one's is a machine
// address. Handing one to the other's pool would be a wild pointer
// dereference in one direction and a nonsense index in the other -- and
// making them separate types turns that into a compile error instead of
// something to detect at runtime, which is this project's whole premise.
//
// WHICH POOL A HANDLE BELONGS TO
//
// The owner's first static parameter is `pool_id: addr` -- the erased
// ADDRESS of the pool that minted it (GitHub issue #345, the same
// mechanism SPEC.md's MutexGuard uses to tie a guard to its lock). Every
// entry point here takes its pool as `&mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id`
// (issue #314's reference type, plus the identity issue #347 taught it
// to carry), so freeing pool A's handle through pool B is a compile
// error:
//
//     intrusive_pool_remove(&pool_b, a0);
//     // static value mismatch: &pool_a vs &pool_b
//
// growable_pool/slotmap cannot see that case at all -- one pool's small
// integer index is a perfectly plausible index in another -- and an
// earlier draft of this file could only count it after the fact.
// IntrusivePoolInsertResult is indexed by the same `pool_id`, which is
// what carries the brand through a FALLIBLE acquire; without indexed
// variants the identity was lost at exactly the boundary an allocator
// lives at.
//
// FORGED ADDRESSES AND STALE HANDLES
//
// What the brand does NOT prove is that the ADDRESS inside a handle is
// really one of this pool's slots: intrusive_owner_into_parts /
// intrusive_owner_new (the round trip a caller wrapping this handle in
// its own owner type needs -- kernel/net/tcp.tkb's TcpConnectionOwner is
// the motivating shape) can pair this pool's brand with another pool's
// address. That now takes explicitly naming both pools, but it is still
// expressible, so intrusive_pool_remove validates before touching any
// memory the handle names: intrusive_pool_slot_is_valid asks the page
// provider which page the address falls in, checks that page's chunk
// header says THIS pool owns it, then checks the offset lands exactly on
// a slot boundary. It never dereferences an address it has not already
// proven belongs to this pool -- the provider answers "is this one of my
// pages" by arithmetic, and the header it hands back is the provider's
// own memory, not memory the suspect address points at. Rejections are
// counted in intrusive_pool_foreign_handle_count. The cost is O(1) since
// issue #349 moved the header out of the page; it used to be O(chunks),
// because finding the header meant walking this pool's chunk list.
//
// A pool that never needs that round trip can have the whole check
// deleted: linearity plus the brand would then prove everything, and
// remove becomes an unconditional O(1) unlink.
//
// A CORRUPTED FREE CHAIN
//
// The free path above is about a handle of unknown provenance. The ALLOC
// path has a separate exposure that no handle is involved in at all
// (GitHub issue #352): intrusive_pool_insert pops `header.free_head`,
// which is a pointer read out of a free slot's own memory. A single stray
// write into that `next_free` word -- or into `free_head` itself -- would
// otherwise make the next insert hand the caller an arbitrary,
// attacker-chosen `*T`. That is the classic freelist-poisoning primitive,
// and it is exactly what Linux SLUB's CONFIG_SLAB_FREELIST_HARDENED
// obfuscates its own chain pointers to blunt.
//
// The intrusive design is what introduces it: an array-indexed pool
// (kernel/lib/freelist.tkb, slotmap.tkb) corrupted the same way yields a
// wrong INDEX, which its own bounds check catches. Here it yields a wrong
// POINTER. So insert validates the popped address before returning it --
// but it needs no chunk walk to do so, because it already knows which
// chunk it is allocating from: intrusive_chunk_slot_in_range is O(1)
// arithmetic on values the chunk header already holds. On failure the
// pointer is not handed out, the event is counted, and that chunk is
// QUARANTINED -- see IntrusiveChunkHeader.corrupt_free_chain_count.
//
// That is two checks, in an order that matters. The arithmetic one proves
// the popped address is a well-formed slot of THIS chunk, which is what
// makes it safe to dereference at all; only then does insert read the
// slot's `generation` and reject a nonzero one, because a live slot on a
// free chain is a contradiction. Without the second check the first would
// still pass an address corrupted to point at a LIVE slot in the same
// chunk -- which does not fault, and so would silently alias two owners
// onto one slot: quieter than the wild pointer, and worse.
//
// Not attempted here, deliberately: XOR-obfuscating the chain pointer
// with a per-pool random cookie, which is real defence in depth but needs
// an entropy source this kernel has no story for yet, and randomizing a
// chunk's initial free order.
//
// WHERE THE LINK LIVES
//
// In the payload's own storage (GitHub issue #348), which is why
// IntrusiveSlot(T) is `{ generation, storage: [u8; sizeof(T)] }` and not
// `{ generation, next_free, payload }`. The link matters only while the
// slot is free and the payload only while it is live, so the two never
// need to coexist -- overlaying them is what SLUB and pool(9) both do,
// and it takes this pool's bookkeeping from 16 bytes per object to 8:
// 248 objects per chunk for an 8-byte payload instead of 165.
//
// `generation` is what says which reading is the valid one, and it was
// already doing that job: 0 means free, which is the invariant the
// alloc-path check above relies on. That makes the slot a tagged union
// whose tag it already had -- spelling it as a `variant` instead
// measures 32 bytes, because the variant adds a SECOND tag.
//
// The gain is 1.50x, not 2x, and only for small payloads: the payload
// itself does not shrink, so a 64-byte one gains 1.12x and a 1008-byte
// one gains nothing at all (#349's in-page header is the whole story at
// that end). `generation` is deliberately not overlaid -- it is live
// exactly when the payload is, so it has no complementary partner, and
// it buys the always-on use-after-free check Linux offers only under
// KASAN.
//
// `[u8; sizeof(T)]` is what makes this expressible: a second `T`-typed
// field would have claimed its own storage instead of sharing. That
// spelling did not compile until commit a81e7f9 -- array sizes folded
// `sizeof` at parse time, which cannot work for a generic's own type
// parameter, since T has no layout until monomorphization binds it.
//
// WHAT A FRESHLY ALLOCATED PAYLOAD CONTAINS
//
// Nothing this pool promises anything about. Write it before reading it
// (GitHub issue #356).
//
// That is a contract, not an accident, and the interesting part is that
// it is UNCONDITIONAL. The earlier behaviour was "zeroed on a slot's
// first use, stale afterwards" -- the worst combination, because code
// written against the first half keeps working until the pool wraps and
// then stops. This pool now zeroes nothing, anywhere: not on free, not
// on reuse, and not at grow time either. The page-clearing loop grow used
// to run is gone, so a chunk costs one page_alloc_contiguous() and the
// carve loop rather than that, a whole-chunk clear, and the carve
// loop. Every field of IntrusiveChunkHeader is written explicitly by
// grow, and every slot gets its generation and its chain link written, so
// the clear was covering nothing that is not covered by construction.
//
// Per-allocation cost of the contract: zero. That is the reason to
// prefer it over "always clean" (which pays sizeof(T) of writes on every
// operation) given that this pool has no way to run a caller's
// constructor. SLAB/UMA's "always constructed" answer is the one that
// adds real capability -- expensive per-object setup paid once per chunk
// instead of once per allocation -- and it is incompatible with the
// overlay above, since a payload cannot stay constructed in storage the
// free chain is using. Choosing the overlay chose this.
//
// What a caller who ignores the contract actually sees, for the record:
// the payload's first word is the free-chain link the slot was carrying,
// and the rest is whatever was last there -- a previous occupant's bytes
// on reuse, and on first use whatever the page provider handed over.
// `pool_u` in the exerciser pins both halves.
//
// One thing this pool does still clear, and it is not a promise to
// anybody: intrusive_pool_release_chunk scrubs a page before handing it
// back to the provider, so a pool's freed objects cannot be read by
// whichever pool is given that page next. That is hygiene owed to the
// data, not a guarantee owed to the next allocation -- see its own
// comment. It is also why removing grow's clear did not simply move a
// cost around: grow paid on every chunk a pool ever creates, release
// pays only when a page actually leaves.
//

// One per chunk, living in the page provider's per-page metadata (GitHub
// issue #349) -- never at offset 0 of the chunk's own page, and never in a
// pool-side array indexed by chunk number.
//
// Two intrusive doubly-linked lists run through these headers:
//
// - the CHUNK list (next_chunk/prev_chunk) threads every chunk this pool
//   owns. It is what intrusive_pool_slot_is_valid walks to decide whether
//   an address of unknown provenance belongs here, and what the iteration
//   cursor walks. Doubly linked so reclaim can unlink in O(1).
// - the PARTIAL list (next_partial/prev_partial, membership flagged by
//   on_partial) threads only the chunks with at least one free slot.
//   Allocation always takes from its head, so it is O(1) and never has to
//   search -- SLUB's own partial-list shape (GitHub issue #346).
//
// `on_partial` is a real field rather than something inferred from the
// links because 0 is a legitimate value for both of them: the only member
// of the partial list has next_partial == prev_partial == 0.
//
// `free_head` is this chunk's OWN free chain, threaded through its free
// slots' `next_free` words. It is per chunk, not global, precisely so
// that a chunk emptying is a local fact -- with one global chain, giving a
// page back would have meant walking every slot of every other chunk to
// unthread this one's.
//
// `corrupt_free_chain_count` (GitHub issue #352) does two jobs with one
// word, because 0 is unambiguously "clean": it counts the free-chain
// entries this chunk offered that failed intrusive_pool_insert's range
// check, and being nonzero IS this chunk's QUARANTINE flag -- see
// intrusive_pool_link_partial, which refuses to re-admit a quarantined
// chunk as an allocation source, and intrusive_pool_remove, which refuses
// to hand its page back. It lives here rather than in IntrusivePool(T)
// for two reasons. It costs nothing: the page provider reserves
// INTRUSIVE_CHUNK_HEADER_BYTES (128) for a header that uses 88, so this
// field consumes room already set aside, and IntrusivePool(T)'s
// advertised 80-byte static footprint does not move (the point of issues
// #348/#349 is that bytes per OBJECT and per PAGE are what scale; a
// per-pool byte is the cheap axis, but this one turned out to be free on
// either). Since issue #350 the reservation is per CHUNK and sits at the
// chunk's far end, so it costs one slot's worth of tail at order 0 and
// proportionally less at every higher order. And a per-chunk
// quarantine flag is needed regardless, since without it the very next
// intrusive_pool_remove into this chunk would link it straight back onto
// the partial list.
struct IntrusiveChunkHeader {
    pool_tag: usize;
    next_chunk: usize;
    prev_chunk: usize;
    next_partial: usize;
    prev_partial: usize;
    on_partial: usize;
    free_head: usize;
    live_count: usize;
    corrupt_free_chain_count: usize;
}

// GitHub issue #348: `storage` is the payload's raw bytes, and it is
// ALSO where the free-chain link lives -- the two are perfectly
// complementary in time, since the link is meaningful only while the slot
// is free and the payload only while it is live. Overlaying them is what
// every production allocator does (SLUB writes its chain pointer into the
// free object; NetBSD's pool(9) overlays `struct pool_item`), and it
// halves this slot's bookkeeping from 16 bytes to 8.
//
// `[u8; sizeof(T)]` is what makes the overlay expressible at all: a
// second field of type `T` would have forced its own storage rather than
// sharing. That spelling needed a compiler change (commit a81e7f9) --
// array_size's `sizeof` used to fold at parse time, which cannot work for
// a generic's own type parameter.
//
// `generation` is deliberately NOT overlaid: it is live while the object
// is, so it has no complementary partner, and its 8 bytes buy the
// always-on use-after-free check that Linux only offers under KASAN. It
// is also what says which of the two readings of `storage` is the valid
// one -- 0 means the link, nonzero means a T -- which makes this slot a
// tagged union whose tag it already was. (Spelling it as a `variant`
// measures 32 bytes rather than 16: the variant adds a second tag
// duplicating this one.)
generic struct IntrusiveSlot(T: type) {
    generation: usize;
    storage: [u8; sizeof(T)];
}

// Scalars only. No array, no N, no MAX_CHUNKS -- this struct's size does
// not depend on how many objects the pool will hold.
//
// `empty_chunk_count` and `retained_chunks` are the hysteresis decision
// issue #346 asked to be made explicit, and issue #354 then made
// adjustable: the pool holds back up to `retained_chunks` fully-empty
// chunks instead of returning every page the moment it empties. A pool
// oscillating around a chunk boundary -- allocate, free, allocate --
// would otherwise hand a page back and immediately ask for another on
// every cycle. One is the smallest cache that removes that thrash and so
// is the default, but it is the caller's to choose now, because whether
// that oscillation happens at all is a property of what the pool holds
// rather than of this file. `empty_chunk_count` is a COUNT rather than
// the single held address it replaced, which is what generalizing to
// more than one required.
generic struct IntrusivePool(T: type) {
    pool_tag: usize;
    // GitHub issue #445: the lock the pool takes. Per POOL, which is the
    // cheapest axis there is -- six pools in this kernel, so 48 bytes in
    // total, against a per-object or per-chunk field that would scale with
    // what the pool holds. pool_tag cannot double as it: a tag is a mixed
    // counter identifying the pool (issue #399), not a location.
    //
    // Issue #451 gave it a type. It was a bare `usize` and there was no
    // way to tell it from the slot addresses and payload addresses around
    // it (issue #396); Mutex is also where a per-lock interrupt LEVEL will
    // go when interrupts get priorities.
    lock: Mutex;
    chunk_head: usize;
    partial_head: usize;
    empty_chunk_count: usize;
    retained_chunks: usize;
    next_generation: usize;
    live_count: usize;
    chunk_count: usize;
    double_remove_count: usize;
    foreign_handle_count: usize;
    released_chunk_count: usize;
}

// GitHub issue #345: `pool_id` is the erased ADDRESS of the pool that
// minted this handle. It costs nothing at runtime and makes "handle from
// pool A, freed through pool B" a compile error rather than something to
// notice afterwards -- see this file's header.
private linear struct IntrusiveOwner[pool_id: addr, allocation: usize] {
    private slot: usize;
    private generation: usize @ allocation;
}

// Indexed by the same `pool_id` its payload carries (GitHub issue #345) --
// without that, the brand would be lost at exactly the boundary a real
// allocator lives at, since a fallible acquire must return a variant.
must_use variant IntrusivePoolInsertResult[pool_id: addr] {
    OutOfMemory;
    Allocated(exists allocation: usize. IntrusiveOwner[pool_id, allocation]);
}

// growable_pool's own grow result has to distinguish THREE
// outcomes (issue #295): its own MAX_CHUNKS ceiling, the global page
// allocator being empty, and success. Two of those three collapse here --
// this pool has no ceiling of its own to reach, and the one remaining
// failure is the genuinely physical one.
must_use variant IntrusivePoolGrowResult {
    OutOfMemory;
    Grown;
}

// A per-chunk record of which pool owns it, for a future reclaim step and
// for debugging a dumped page -- NOT the cross-pool check, which is the
// erased `pool_id` brand and costs nothing at runtime (see this file's
// header). A plain counter rather than the pool's own address: an address
// would need an unsafe cast in what is otherwise a fully safe entry
// point.
private let mut intrusive_pool_next_tag: usize = 1;

// GitHub issue #399: a pool tag is not 1, 2, 3, ...
//
// intrusive_pool_owns_chunk decides "is this page one of MY chunks" by
// comparing the header's pool_tag with the pool's own, and a region whose
// bytes happen to hold a small integer at the header offset had a
// non-trivial chance of reading as some pool's chunk. Two things already
// narrow that a lot -- issue #384's allocator-owned page tag is consulted
// FIRST, without dereferencing the address at all, and issue #350 moved
// the header outside the carved slot range so no legitimate payload write
// reaches it -- leaving the residual case of a page genuinely tagged
// PoolChunk whose header region was written by something misbehaving.
// This subsystem has already paid once for "low probability, no
// detection" (#373), and closing it costs two multiplies.
//
// The counter stays as the source of uniqueness and is MIXED, rather than
// replaced by the pool's own address, for two reasons:
//
// - Re-initialising a pool must produce a DIFFERENT tag. That is what
//   makes chunks a re-init forgot stop matching (see
//   intrusive_pool_init_retaining, and issue #257's page-leak incident
//   for what forgetting them means). An address repeats.
// - A kernel address is precisely the byte pattern a corrupted region is
//   most likely to contain. Issue #373's diagnosis started from
//   recognising repeated kernel addresses on a 16-byte stride, which is
//   the opposite of what a tag should look like.
//
// splitmix64's finaliser: every input bit avalanches, so consecutive
// counter values land far apart. It is a BIJECTION on 64 bits, which is
// what keeps the counter's uniqueness -- distinct pools keep distinct
// tags, and the mixing buys unpredictability without buying collisions.
//
// 0 is the one value that must not be handed out ("no pool", so a zeroed
// header never matches), and exactly one input maps to it. That input is
// answered with 1, which is the only collision this introduces: between
// two specific 64-bit preimages that a counter starting at 1 and stepping
// by 1 does not reach in any imaginable run. `| 1` was the first draft and
// is worse -- it folds every tag onto an odd value, halving the space for
// every pool rather than for one.
private fn intrusive_pool_mix_tag(value: usize) -> usize {
    let mut x: usize = value ^ 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15;
    x = (x ^ (x >> 30)) * 0xBF58476D1CE4E5B9;
    x = (x ^ (x >> 27)) * 0x94D049BB133111EB;
    x = x ^ (x >> 31);
    if (x == 0) { return 1; }
    return x;
}

fn intrusive_owner_new(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                       slot: usize, generation: usize @ allocation)
        -> IntrusiveOwner[pool_id, allocation] {
    let mut owner: IntrusiveOwner[pool_id, allocation] = { slot, generation };
    return owner;
}

fn intrusive_owner_slot(owner: borrow IntrusiveOwner[pool_id, allocation]) -> usize {
    return owner.slot;
}

fn intrusive_owner_generation(owner: borrow IntrusiveOwner[pool_id, allocation]) -> usize {
    return owner.generation;
}

// The same escape hatch kernel/lib/freelist.tkb's freelist_owner_into_parts
// provides, for a caller that immediately rewraps the pair in its own
// owner type (kernel/net/tcp.tkb's TcpConnectionOwner is the motivating
// shape). Reconstruct with intrusive_owner_new. This is the ONLY way to
// produce a handle this pool did not mint, which is why remove validates.
fn intrusive_owner_into_parts(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                              owner: sink IntrusiveOwner[pool_id, allocation])
        -> (usize, usize @ allocation) {
    return (owner.slot, owner.generation);
}

// Discharges the linear obligation without returning the slot -- for a
// caller that has already established the slot must not be pushed back
// (see intrusive_pool_remove's own rejection paths).
fn intrusive_owner_drop(owner: sink IntrusiveOwner[pool_id, allocation]) {}

// Retains one empty chunk, which is the anti-thrash default issue #346
// argued for and what every existing caller expects.
// -- Mutual exclusion -------------------------------------------------
// GitHub issue #351. Every pool primitive in this tree carries a "NOT
// SYNCHRONIZED" caveat in a comment, and a comment is exactly the wrong
// place for it: the failure mode is a library that works by accident
// until a caller who is not single-context adopts it, and here a
// corrupted free chain is a wild pointer rather than a wrong index.
//
// So the guard is a TYPE, not a note. Every entry point that reads or
// writes this pool's own state takes `borrow IntrusivePoolGuard[pool_id]`,
// and the only way to obtain one is intrusive_pool_lock on THIS pool.
// Calling unguarded is a missing argument; calling with another pool's
// guard is a static value mismatch. Both are compile errors.
//
// The guard is an erased view, so it costs nothing at runtime -- it is
// the same mechanism SPEC.md's MutexGuard uses, and the same one
// kernel/kernel/process.tkb's ScheduledProcessGuard already uses for the
// process table.
//
// WHAT THIS PROVES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
//
// It proves the DISCIPLINE: no path reaches pool state without having
// acquired, and every acquire is released exactly once on every path,
// because the view is linear. It does not by itself provide mutual
// EXCLUSION -- that is whatever intrusive_pool_lock's body does, and in
// this prototype the body is empty, exactly as ScheduledProcessGuard's
// is today. Porting has to fill it in, and the honest bar is that
// nothing else in this file needs to change when it does.
//
// GitHub issue #445 filled it in, and the bar held: pool_lock_acquire is
// a real spin_lock_irqsave now -- mask, then take an atomic exchange on
// this pool's own lock word -- and nothing in this file changed except
// passing that word's address beside the tag.
//
// USABLE FROM AN INTERRUPT HANDLER, DELIBERATELY
//
// The kernel's exclusion primitive was DAIF.I masking alone while there
// was one core, which is what intrusive_pool_lock became there; issue
// #445 added the acquire under it without changing this shape. That flavour of lock is
// safe to take from BOTH thread and interrupt context -- Linux's
// spin_lock_irqsave -- so this pool composes with `!{interrupt}` rather
// than being excluded from it.
//
// That is the choice, and the alternative is worth naming because the
// compiler would have enforced it for free: marking intrusive_pool_lock
// `!{may_block}` would make every `!{interrupt}` handler that reaches it
// a compile error, since SPEC.md's effect rules say an `interrupt` root's
// reachable call graph must not contain `may_block`. That is the right
// answer for a SLEEPING lock and the wrong one here, because this issue
// exists precisely to unblock adoption in interrupt-reachable paths. A
// lock that cannot be taken from an interrupt handler would have to be
// taken with interrupts already masked by the caller, which is the same
// masking one layer up and one place easier to forget.
// A linear STRUCT, not an erased view, and the reason is the same one
// issue #348's slot permission hit: an erased view has no runtime
// payload, so it cannot carry anything -- and a lock that RESTORES the
// interrupt mask rather than unconditionally enabling it has to carry
// the mask it found. That is Linux's `local_irq_save(flags)`, where
// `flags` lives in the caller's frame; here the guard is that frame slot.
//
// Storing it globally instead was the first design and it is racy: a
// thread that reads the mask, is interrupted before it can set it, and
// resumes after a handler has overwritten the global would restore the
// handler's value and leave interrupts masked forever. Per-guard state
// has no such window, needs no depth counter, and makes nesting fall out
// -- an inner acquire records "already masked" and its release does
// nothing, while the outermost records the truth and restores it.
//
// The cost is that the guard stops being free: 8 stack bytes while held,
// which is the per-ACQUIRE axis and the cheapest one. What it does NOT
// cost is the identity checking -- `[pool_id: addr]` still makes another
// pool's guard a compile error, exactly as it did when this was a view.
private linear struct IntrusivePoolGuard[pool_id: addr] {
    private flags: usize;
}

// Acquires this pool. The body delegates to pool_lock_acquire, which the
// FILE LIST supplies (issue #364): linux_user gives it a checking
// implementation that catches re-entrancy and inconsistent lock order
// (issue #365), and a kernel build gives it a real spinlock inside the
// interrupt mask (issue #445). This file does not know or care which,
// which is what lets the checking be thorough where it is free and absent
// where it would cost.
//
// It passes both the tag and the address of this pool's own lock word.
// The tag is identity, for the checking backend; the address is the thing
// the kernel backend swaps. They are different questions and a tag cannot
// answer the second one.
//
// Deliberately NOT `!{may_block}`: see above.
fn intrusive_pool_lock(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id)
        -> IntrusivePoolGuard[pool_id] {
    let mut guard: IntrusivePoolGuard[pool_id] =
        { pool_lock_acquire(pool.pool_tag, &(*pool).lock) };
    return guard;
}

// Releases it. Linear, so forgetting this is a compile error on every
// path, including early returns -- which is the half of lock discipline
// that review comments are worst at catching.
fn intrusive_pool_unlock(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                         guard: sink IntrusivePoolGuard[pool_id]) {
    pool_lock_release(pool.pool_tag, &(*pool).lock, guard.flags);
}

fn intrusive_pool_init(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id) {
    intrusive_pool_init_retaining(pool, 1);
}

// GitHub issue #354: the reserve is a per-pool policy now, not a constant
// baked into this file. How much churn a pool sees is a property of what
// it holds -- a pool oscillating across a chunk boundary wants a reserve,
// a pool that fills once and drains once at shutdown wants none -- and
// this file cannot know which. 0 gives every page back the moment a chunk
// empties.
//
// A runtime field rather than a generic value parameter, deliberately.
// It is policy, not layout: nothing about the pool's shape depends on it,
// so making it a type parameter would split one type into many and put a
// number back in a caller's type signature, which is what #344's whole
// design fought to remove. It costs the pool struct 8 bytes, which is the
// per-POOL axis and the cheap one -- see this file's header.
fn intrusive_pool_init_retaining(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                                 retained_chunks: usize) {
    pool.pool_tag = intrusive_pool_mix_tag(intrusive_pool_next_tag);
    intrusive_pool_next_tag = intrusive_pool_next_tag + 1;
    // Free. Re-initialising a pool that some core is inside would be a bug
    // this cannot detect, and is not a thing any caller does: init runs
    // once, before the pool is reachable.
    mutex_init(&(*pool).lock);
    pool.chunk_head = 0;
    pool.partial_head = 0;
    pool.empty_chunk_count = 0;
    pool.retained_chunks = retained_chunks;
    pool.next_generation = 1;
    pool.live_count = 0;
    pool.chunk_count = 0;
    pool.double_remove_count = 0;
    pool.foreign_handle_count = 0;
    pool.released_chunk_count = 0;
}

// Both derived from sizeof after monomorphization, never supplied by a
// caller -- see this file's header.
fn intrusive_pool_slot_stride(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id) -> usize {
    return sizeof(IntrusiveSlot(T));
}

// GitHub issue #350: a chunk is 2^order contiguous pages, not one page.
//
// One page meant a hard ceiling -- a T whose slot exceeded a page's
// payload could not be pooled at all, and had to fall back to allocating
// whole pages by hand, which is the pattern this primitive exists to
// replace -- and poor amortization for large T, where a 1960-byte payload
// got 2 slots per chunk and paid a whole header, two list memberships and
// the reclaim bookkeeping over them.
//
// SLUB's `slub_max_order` defaults to 3 (32 KB) and its `slub_min_objects`
// pushes the order up until a slab holds enough objects; UMA does the
// equivalent. These are the same two numbers.
// Two things at once, and the name says which is which because the older
// one (MAX_CHUNK_PAGES) read as "a limit on pages" and was mistaken for
// one:
//
//  - a POLICY knob -- how large one chunk may grow. Not a ceiling on
//    anything: a pool may hold any number of chunks, so this bounds the
//    slab size, not the memory.
//  - a real functional CEILING on sizeof(T), asserted in
//    intrusive_pool_slots_per_chunk: a T whose slot exceeds
//    8 * PAGE_SIZE - INTRUSIVE_CHUNK_HEADER_BYTES (32640 bytes) cannot be
//    pooled at all. Issue #350 raised that from one page's payload to
//    this; it did not remove it. The largest T pooled today is
//    NetFrame at ~1520 bytes.
const INTRUSIVE_MAX_PAGES_PER_CHUNK: usize = 8;
const INTRUSIVE_MIN_SLOTS_PER_CHUNK: usize = 8;

// The chunk header now lives at the END of the chunk rather than in the
// page provider's per-page metadata (which issue #349 put it in, and
// which a multi-page run has none of by construction -- see #377). The
// reservation is per CHUNK, so at order 0 it costs what the provider's
// per-page region used to and at higher orders it costs the same 128
// bytes over 2, 4 or 8 pages.
const INTRUSIVE_CHUNK_HEADER_BYTES: usize = 128;

// How many pages back one chunk of this pool: the smallest power of two
// holding INTRUSIVE_MIN_SLOTS_PER_CHUNK slots, capped at
// INTRUSIVE_MAX_PAGES_PER_CHUNK. Every operand is a compile-time property of
// T, so this folds to a constant per instantiation -- it is a function
// rather than a `const` only because `const` cannot depend on `sizeof(T)`
// before monomorphization.
//
// The cap is a cap, not a requirement: a T so large that even 8 pages
// hold fewer than the minimum still pools, with however many slots fit.
// What it may not do is fail to fit ONE slot, which is a static_assert in
// intrusive_pool_slots_per_chunk.
fn intrusive_pool_chunk_pages(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id)
        -> usize {
    let stride: usize = sizeof(IntrusiveSlot(T));
    let mut pages: usize = 1;
    while (pages < INTRUSIVE_MAX_PAGES_PER_CHUNK &&
           (pages * PAGE_SIZE - INTRUSIVE_CHUNK_HEADER_BYTES) / stride <
               INTRUSIVE_MIN_SLOTS_PER_CHUNK) {
        pages = pages * 2;
    }
    return pages;
}

// The bytes one chunk spans, which is also its ALIGNMENT: a power-of-two
// page run from page_alloc_contiguous is aligned to its own size, and
// that is what keeps intrusive_chunk_base_of an O(1) mask rather than a
// search. Issue #380 aligned runs for exactly this kind of consumer.
fn intrusive_pool_chunk_bytes(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id)
        -> usize {
    return intrusive_pool_chunk_pages(pool) * PAGE_SIZE;
}

fn intrusive_pool_slots_per_chunk(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id) -> usize {
    // GitHub issue #344: "does a T-sized slot fit in a chunk at all?" is a
    // property of T and of this file's own layout constants, both fully
    // known once monomorphization has picked T -- so it is settled here,
    // once per instantiation, and a pool of an oversized T does not
    // compile. An earlier draft of this file carried a SlotTooLarge case
    // through IntrusivePoolGrowResult and IntrusivePoolInsertResult
    // instead, making every CALLER match on a runtime outcome that could
    // never actually occur for a T that compiled once.
    // GitHub issue #350: the ceiling is now the LARGEST chunk, not one
    // page, so what has to hold is that a slot fits in 8 pages minus the
    // header. A T under about 32 KB pools; the one thing that still does
    // not compile is a T too large for any chunk this file will build.
    static_assert(sizeof(IntrusiveSlot(T)) <=
                      INTRUSIVE_MAX_PAGES_PER_CHUNK * PAGE_SIZE -
                      INTRUSIVE_CHUNK_HEADER_BYTES,
                  "IntrusivePool(T): one T-sized slot does not fit in the largest chunk this pool will build");
    // The header's reservation is this file's own now, so this is a check
    // that IT reserved enough rather than that the provider did.
    static_assert(sizeof(IntrusiveChunkHeader) <= INTRUSIVE_CHUNK_HEADER_BYTES,
                  "IntrusivePool(T): the per-chunk header reservation is smaller than a chunk header");
    // intrusive_pool_chunk_pages doubles from 1 and stops at
    // `pages < INTRUSIVE_MAX_PAGES_PER_CHUNK`, so a cap that is not a power of
    // two is not a cap: at 6 the sequence runs 1, 2, 4, 8 and leaves 8.
    // The alignment page_alloc_contiguous promises -- which is what makes
    // intrusive_chunk_base_of an O(1) mask -- holds only for power-of-two
    // runs, so overshooting the cap would silently produce chunks whose
    // base cannot be recovered from a slot address.
    static_assert((INTRUSIVE_MAX_PAGES_PER_CHUNK &
                   (INTRUSIVE_MAX_PAGES_PER_CHUNK - 1)) == 0,
                  "INTRUSIVE_MAX_PAGES_PER_CHUNK must be a power of two, or the order search overshoots it and page_alloc_contiguous stops promising alignment");
    // GitHub issue #348, the two conditions the payload/link overlay
    // needs. Both are properties of T alone, so both are settled here
    // rather than being anything a caller or a runtime path can get
    // wrong -- the same stance the two asserts above take.
    //
    // 1. The link has to fit in the storage it shares with the payload.
    //    A T smaller than a pointer would have the chain write past its
    //    own slot and into the next one's generation word.
    static_assert(sizeof(T) >= sizeof(usize),
                  "IntrusivePool(T): T is smaller than the free-chain link that shares its storage");
    // 2. `storage` is a `[u8; N]`, whose own alignment is 1, so it
    //    contributes nothing to IntrusiveSlot(T)'s alignment -- unlike
    //    the `payload: T` field it replaced, which used to force it. The
    //    slot is therefore aligned to `generation`'s 8, and a T wanting
    //    more than that would be handed a misaligned address. Stated
    //    against `alignof(usize)` rather than a literal 8, because 8 is
    //    not the constant that matters -- what the slot guarantees is
    //    exactly `generation`'s own alignment, and saying so keeps this
    //    correct on a target where that is not 8.
    //
    //    No T can violate this TODAY, and the assert is deliberately here
    //    anyway. Every takibi type currently tops out at 8-byte alignment
    //    (the widest primitive is 8; a struct takes the max of its
    //    fields; an array takes its element's), and `align(N)` does not
    //    raise a struct's alignment where it is embedded -- `struct A
    //    align(32) {...}` inside `struct H { pad: u8; v: A; }` still
    //    lands at offset 8, though SPEC.md's "every instance/array
    //    element aligned to N" reads as though it should not. So this
    //    assert is unfalsifiable until over-alignment becomes
    //    expressible, and it is exactly then that a silently misaligned
    //    payload would appear here. Cheaper to write it now, while the
    //    reasoning is in hand, than to remember later.
    static_assert(alignof(T) <= alignof(usize),
                  "IntrusivePool(T): T needs stronger alignment than the slot layout guarantees");
    return (intrusive_pool_chunk_bytes(pool) - INTRUSIVE_CHUNK_HEADER_BYTES) /
           sizeof(IntrusiveSlot(T));
}

// The chunk a slot address belongs to. Every chunk is a power-of-two page
// run aligned to its own size (page_alloc_contiguous's promise, issue
// #380), so this stays an O(1) mask at every order -- spelled as
// arithmetic to keep it readable. A slot in the fifth page of an
// eight-page chunk answers exactly as one in the first.
fn intrusive_chunk_base_of(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                           slot_address: usize) -> usize {
    let span: usize = intrusive_pool_chunk_bytes(pool);
    if (span == 0) { return slot_address; }
    return slot_address - (slot_address % span);
}

// The chunk header, at the END of the chunk's own run (GitHub issue
// #350). It lived in the provider's per-page metadata region from issue
// #349 until now; a multi-page run has no such region by construction --
// issue #377 made a run entirely payload precisely so that no pool could
// read a kernel stack's frames as a chunk header -- so the header came
// back into the chunk, at its tail, where the carve below stops short of
// it.
//
// What replaces the safety #349 got from the allocator's own
// arithmetic-only bounds check (page_meta_at, deleted by issue #390 once
// this file stopped being its only consumer) is stronger, not weaker:
// intrusive_pool_owns_chunk now asks the ALLOCATOR whether the page is
// tagged PoolChunk (issue #384) before anything here is dereferenced.
// That tag is allocator-owned per-page state, unforgeable by a consumer,
// and it is checked without reading through `base` at all -- so a garbage
// address is rejected before this function is reached, rather than
// yielding a harmless-but-wrong header afterwards.
//
// Callers pass either a chunk base this pool recorded at grow time, or
// one intrusive_pool_owns_chunk has just accepted.
fn intrusive_chunk_header_at(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                             base: usize) -> *IntrusiveChunkHeader !{unsafe} {
    return unsafe {
        (base + intrusive_pool_chunk_bytes(pool) -
         INTRUSIVE_CHUNK_HEADER_BYTES) as *IntrusiveChunkHeader
    };
}

// Same for the slot side. Callers hold either an address they just
// computed from a chunk they own, or one validated by
// intrusive_pool_slot_is_valid.
fn intrusive_slot_at(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id, slot_address: usize)
        -> *IntrusiveSlot(T) !{unsafe} {
    return unsafe { slot_address as *IntrusiveSlot(T) };
}

// The free-chain link (GitHub issue #348). This is the SAME address
// intrusive_pool_payload_at hands out as a `*T` -- that is the overlay,
// stated in one place instead of being spread across the three sites
// that thread the chain. Which of the two readings is the valid one is
// never guessed: `generation == 0` says link, nonzero says payload, and
// every caller here is on a path that has just established which.
//
// Both readings are ordinary raw casts at this file's existing
// provenance boundary -- the same one intrusive_slot_at and
// intrusive_pool_payload_at already sit on -- so the overlay adds no new
// class of unchecked operation, only a second view of storage whose
// exclusivity the generation word already tracked.
fn intrusive_slot_link_at(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                          slot_address: usize) -> *usize !{unsafe} {
    return unsafe {
        (slot_address + offsetof(IntrusiveSlot(T), storage)) as *usize
    };
}

// Is `base` a chunk this pool actually owns? O(1) since GitHub issue
// #349, and safer than the O(chunks) list walk it replaces rather than
// merely faster.
//
// The old walk existed to avoid reading a header at `base` when `base`
// might be garbage -- the header lived IN the page, so trusting it was
// the wild dereference being guarded against. The header is back in the
// chunk since issue #350, and the inversion still holds, because what is
// consulted FIRST is not the header: page_owner_tag_at bounds-checks
// `base` against the page range and reads the allocator's own per-page
// tag, dereferencing `base` not at all. Only a page the allocator says
// is somebody's pool chunk gets read through. So there is still no
// moment where an unproven address is trusted, and the answer costs a
// division and two comparisons instead of a list walk.
//
// The tag is the authority, twice over: the allocator's says whether the
// page is any pool's chunk, and the header's pool_tag says WHICH pool's.
// A tag is a mixed counter value and never 0 (issue #399), so a zeroed
// header never matches a live pool
// even if a page were somehow tagged without one.
fn intrusive_pool_owns_chunk(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id, base: usize)
        -> bool !{unsafe} {
    // The allocator's own per-page tag, read without dereferencing
    // `base` (issue #384). A page that is not currently somebody's pool
    // chunk cannot pass this, whatever its contents say -- which is what
    // lets the header live inside the chunk again without reintroducing
    // the forged-header hazard.
    if (page_owner_tag_at(base) != PageOwnerTag::PoolChunk) { return false; }
    return intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, base).pool_tag == pool.pool_tag;
}

// Full validation of a raw slot address: it names a chunk on this pool's
// list, and it lands exactly on a slot boundary within that chunk's carved
// slot range. After this returns true, dereferencing the slot is sound.
// It is exactly intrusive_chunk_slot_in_range below, plus the chunk-list
// walk that finds out WHICH chunk to range-check against. Stated that way
// rather than repeating the arithmetic, because these two are the file's
// only validation of a raw address and two copies of it would eventually
// disagree -- and the copy that drifts is the one deciding whether a
// dereference is sound.
fn intrusive_pool_slot_is_valid(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                                slot_address: usize) -> bool !{unsafe} {
    if (slot_address == 0) { return false; }
    let base: usize = intrusive_chunk_base_of(pool, slot_address);
    if (intrusive_pool_owns_chunk(pool, base) == false) { return false; }
    return intrusive_chunk_slot_in_range(pool, base, slot_address);
}

// GitHub issue #352: the O(1) core of validation, and the alloc fast
// path's whole check. Does `slot_address` land in the chunk at `base`, on
// an exact slot boundary, below the number of slots a chunk holds?
//
// intrusive_pool_slot_is_valid above wraps this in the "which chunk, and
// is it mine" step, because it starts from an address of UNKNOWN
// provenance. intrusive_pool_insert needs none of that: the address under
// suspicion is its own chunk's `free_head`, so `base` is already in hand
// and this is pure arithmetic.
//
// GitHub issue #361: the two bounds are DERIVED, not read. They used to
// be `header.slot_stride` and `header.slot_count`, which made a stray
// write into the header weaken the very check meant to catch stray
// writes -- a `slot_stride` of 1 makes almost every address in the page
// look slot-aligned. Both are compile-time constants after
// monomorphization, so loading them was never buying anything: this is
// the same "delete the corruptible copy rather than check it" move that
// removed SLOTS_PER_CHUNK from the caller's hands in the first place.
// There is now no failure path for a corrupted bound, because there is no
// stored bound.
//
// `base` is still trusted to be a real chunk base: either the caller
// proved it (slot_is_valid) or it is reading its own partial-list head.
fn intrusive_chunk_slot_in_range(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                                 base: usize, slot_address: usize) -> bool !{unsafe} {
    if (slot_address < base) { return false; }
    // Slots start at the chunk's own offset 0 -- the header sits at the
    // far END of the chunk (issue #350) -- so the offset IS the slot
    // offset, at every order.
    let slot_offset: usize = slot_address - base;
    let stride: usize = sizeof(IntrusiveSlot(T));
    if (slot_offset % stride != 0) { return false; }
    return slot_offset / stride < intrusive_pool_slots_per_chunk(pool);
}

// -- The two chunk lists -----------------------------------------------
// All four of these are O(1); both lists are doubly linked precisely so
// that reclaim (issue #346) never has to search for the chunk it is about
// to unlink.

fn intrusive_pool_link_partial(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                               base: usize) !{unsafe} {
    let header: *IntrusiveChunkHeader = intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, base);
    if (header.on_partial != 0) { return; }
    // GitHub issue #352: a quarantined chunk never becomes an allocation
    // source again. This is the guard that makes the quarantine stick --
    // allocation only ever takes from the partial list, and every path
    // that would re-admit a chunk (notably intrusive_pool_remove freeing a
    // slot back into it) goes through here.
    if (header.corrupt_free_chain_count != 0) { return; }
    header.prev_partial = 0;
    header.next_partial = pool.partial_head;
    if (pool.partial_head != 0) {
        intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, pool.partial_head).prev_partial = base;
    }
    pool.partial_head = base;
    header.on_partial = 1;
}

fn intrusive_pool_unlink_partial(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                                 base: usize) !{unsafe} {
    let header: *IntrusiveChunkHeader = intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, base);
    if (header.on_partial == 0) { return; }
    if (header.prev_partial != 0) {
        intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, header.prev_partial).next_partial = header.next_partial;
    } else {
        pool.partial_head = header.next_partial;
    }
    if (header.next_partial != 0) {
        intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, header.next_partial).prev_partial = header.prev_partial;
    }
    header.prev_partial = 0;
    header.next_partial = 0;
    header.on_partial = 0;
}

fn intrusive_pool_unlink_chunk(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                               base: usize) !{unsafe} {
    let header: *IntrusiveChunkHeader = intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, base);
    if (header.prev_chunk != 0) {
        intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, header.prev_chunk).next_chunk = header.next_chunk;
    } else {
        pool.chunk_head = header.next_chunk;
    }
    if (header.next_chunk != 0) {
        intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, header.next_chunk).prev_chunk = header.prev_chunk;
    }
    header.prev_chunk = 0;
    header.next_chunk = 0;
}

// Hands one fully-empty chunk's page back to the provider. Both lists are
// unlinked first, so the moment this returns, intrusive_pool_slot_is_valid
// already rejects every address in that page -- which is what keeps a
// stale handle into a reclaimed chunk from being dereferenced. If the
// SAME page later comes back as a chunk of this same pool, the addresses
// become valid again, and the pool-global (never per-chunk) generation
// counter is what still rejects the stale handle.
fn intrusive_pool_release_chunk(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                                base: usize) !{unsafe} {
    intrusive_pool_unlink_partial(pool, base);
    intrusive_pool_unlink_chunk(pool, base);
    pool.chunk_count = pool.chunk_count - 1;
    pool.released_chunk_count = pool.released_chunk_count + 1;
    // Scrub before handing the page over. The payloads that lived here
    // belonged to THIS pool, and once the page is back in the provider's
    // supply any other pool can be given it -- so without this, one
    // pool's freed objects are readable as another pool's freshly
    // allocated ones. Verified: a pool that filled and drained two chunks
    // left 0xDEAD/0xBEEF/0xCAFE visible in the next pool's first
    // allocation.
    //
    // Release is the right place for it rather than grow, which is where
    // an equivalent clear used to sit (removed for issue #356). Grow pays
    // on every chunk a pool ever creates; release pays only when a page
    // actually leaves, which is rarer in a growing pool and equal at
    // worst. It also puts the cost on the component that knows the data
    // was sensitive, instead of making every future consumer of a page
    // defensively clear it.
    //
    // This does NOT weaken issue #356's contract. A freshly allocated
    // payload is still whatever the provider supplied -- this pool
    // promises nothing about it, and a page arriving from the provider
    // for the first time was never touched here at all.
    //
    // If the page provider ever grows a scrub-on-free policy of its own
    // (Linux's init_on_free, which lives in the page allocator rather
    // than in each slab cache), this becomes redundant and should go.
    intrusive_pool_scrub_chunk(pool, base);
    // The chunk base IS the run's physical address, which is what the
    // provider's transferred-run release entry point takes -- this pool
    // gave up its PageRunOwner at grow time, keeping only the address and
    // knowing the count from T (GitHub issue #350).
    page_run_transferred_release_physical(base, intrusive_pool_chunk_pages(pool));
}

private fn intrusive_pool_scrub_chunk(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                                      base: usize) !{unsafe} {
    let bytes: *u8 = unsafe { base as *u8 };
    // The WHOLE chunk, header included: the header names this pool, and a
    // page going back to the provider carrying a live-looking pool_tag is
    // the forged-header hazard with the roles reversed.
    let span: isize = intrusive_pool_chunk_bytes(pool) as isize;
    for i: isize in 0..<span {
        bytes[i] = 0;
    }
}

// Pulls one more page, carves it into slots, threads them onto that
// chunk's OWN free chain, and puts the chunk at the head of the partial
// list -- the pool's only growth step. Slots are linked in descending
// order so the chain hands them out in ascending order, which keeps a
// chunk's first-fill behavior easy to reason about (contrast
// growable_pool_activate_chunk's own ascending push / descending pop).
fn intrusive_pool_grow_one_chunk(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id)
        -> IntrusivePoolGrowResult !{unsafe} {
    let stride: usize = sizeof(IntrusiveSlot(T));
    let slot_count: usize = intrusive_pool_slots_per_chunk(pool);
    match page_alloc_contiguous(intrusive_pool_chunk_pages(pool),
                                PageOwnerTag::PoolChunk) {
        PageRunAllocResult::OutOfMemory => {
            return IntrusivePoolGrowResult::OutOfMemory;
        }
        PageRunAllocResult::Allocated(run_owner) => {
            let base: usize = page_run_physical_address(run_owner);
            // The pool keeps only the address from here on; the run's own
            // owner is given up to this coarser bookkeeping, and
            // intrusive_pool_release_chunk is what eventually gives the
            // pages themselves back.
            let _run_index: usize = page_run_owner_transfer_index(run_owner) as usize;
            let header: *IntrusiveChunkHeader = intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, base);
            header.pool_tag = pool.pool_tag;
            header.next_chunk = pool.chunk_head;
            header.prev_chunk = 0;
            header.next_partial = 0;
            header.prev_partial = 0;
            header.on_partial = 0;
            header.free_head = 0;
            header.live_count = 0;
            header.corrupt_free_chain_count = 0;
            if (pool.chunk_head != 0) {
                intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, pool.chunk_head).prev_chunk = base;
            }
            pool.chunk_head = base;
            pool.chunk_count = pool.chunk_count + 1;
            let mut remaining: usize = slot_count;
            while (remaining > 0) {
                remaining = remaining - 1;
                let slot_address: usize =
                    base + remaining * stride;
                let slot: *IntrusiveSlot(T) = intrusive_slot_at(pool, slot_address);
                slot.generation = 0;
                // generation is 0, so this slot's storage is the link's,
                // not a payload's (GitHub issue #348).
                *intrusive_slot_link_at(pool, slot_address) = header.free_head;
                header.free_head = slot_address;
            }
            intrusive_pool_link_partial(pool, base);
            return IntrusivePoolGrowResult::Grown;
        }
    }
}

// Always allocates from the head of the partial list, which by
// construction has a free slot -- O(1), no search over chunks (GitHub
// issue #346).
// GitHub issue #369: the same allocation, with the payload's bytes
// zeroed before the handle is returned.
//
// A T that contains STABLE OWNER STORAGE -- a `private` field whose type
// is a linear variant, the one durable home a linear value has -- must be
// allocated through this and not through intrusive_pool_insert. The
// reason is that the language's empty state for such a field is
// "declaration-order tag zero", inherited from a zero-initialized global
// rather than written by anyone. A pool slot is neither zero-initialized
// nor fresh: reuse it and the next stable_replace reads the previous
// occupant's tag and hands back a linear value no allocation ever
// produced. Demonstrated, not theorised -- see issue #369.
//
// It is a separate entry point rather than a change to insert because the
// cost is only owed by types that hold linear handles. Everything else
// keeps issue #356's contract exactly: a freshly allocated payload is
// uninitialized, and this pool does not invent values for a T it knows
// nothing about.
//
// The requirement is DOCUMENTED, not checked. The compiler has the
// predicate -- type_inf.ml's contains_stable_owner_value -- but it is not
// reachable from a static_assert, so nothing yet rejects allocating such
// a T through the plain insert. Making it checked is what would let this
// be a guarantee rather than a discipline; until then this comment and
// issue #369 are the whole of the enforcement.
fn intrusive_pool_insert_zeroed(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                                guard: borrow IntrusivePoolGuard[pool_id])
        -> IntrusivePoolInsertResult[pool_id] !{unsafe} {
    match intrusive_pool_insert_unchecked(pool, guard) {
        IntrusivePoolInsertResult::OutOfMemory => {
            return IntrusivePoolInsertResult::OutOfMemory;
        }
        IntrusivePoolInsertResult::Allocated(owner) => {
            let bytes: *u8 = unsafe {
                (intrusive_owner_slot(owner)
                 + offsetof(IntrusiveSlot(T), storage)) as *u8
            };
            let span: isize = sizeof(T) as isize;
            for i: isize in 0..<span {
                bytes[i] = 0;
            }
            return IntrusivePoolInsertResult::Allocated(owner);
        }
    }
}

fn intrusive_pool_insert(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                         guard: borrow IntrusivePoolGuard[pool_id])
        -> IntrusivePoolInsertResult[pool_id] !{unsafe} {
    // GitHub issue #369: a T holding STABLE OWNER STORAGE cannot come
    // through here, because its empty state is "declaration-order tag
    // zero" inherited from a zero-initialized global -- and a recycled
    // slot is neither zero nor fresh. Reuse one and the next
    // stable_replace reads the previous occupant's tag and hands back a
    // linear value no allocation ever produced.
    //
    // This used to be a sentence in the limitations header. It is a
    // compile error now: intrusive_pool_insert_zeroed is the entry point
    // that makes the premise true, and the cost is charged only to the
    // types that owe it.
    static_assert(contains_stable_owner(T) == 0,
                  "IntrusivePool(T): a T holding stable owner storage must be allocated with intrusive_pool_insert_zeroed, whose zeroing is what its empty state depends on");
    return intrusive_pool_insert_unchecked(pool, guard);
}

// The allocation itself. Private, and without the assertion above,
// because intrusive_pool_insert_zeroed is the sanctioned way for a T that
// would fail it -- routing that through the checked entry point would
// reject the one caller that is allowed.
private fn intrusive_pool_insert_unchecked(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                                           guard: borrow IntrusivePoolGuard[pool_id])
        -> IntrusivePoolInsertResult[pool_id] !{unsafe} {
    if (pool.partial_head == 0) {
        match intrusive_pool_grow_one_chunk(pool) {
            IntrusivePoolGrowResult::OutOfMemory => {
                return IntrusivePoolInsertResult::OutOfMemory;
            }
            IntrusivePoolGrowResult::Grown => {}
        }
    }
    let base: usize = pool.partial_head;
    if (base == 0) {
        // Unreachable: a chunk that grew successfully was linked into the
        // partial list with slot_count >= 2 free slots. No trap site
        // either way.
        return IntrusivePoolInsertResult::OutOfMemory;
    }
    let header: *IntrusiveChunkHeader = intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, base);
    let slot_address: usize = header.free_head;
    if (slot_address == 0) {
        // Also unreachable: partial-list membership IS "free_head != 0".
        return IntrusivePoolInsertResult::OutOfMemory;
    }
    // GitHub issue #352: a stray write into this free slot's own
    // `next_free` word, or into the chunk header's `free_head`, would
    // otherwise hand the caller an arbitrary `*T` with no check at all --
    // the freelist-poisoning primitive SLUB's hardened configs exist to
    // block. Reject it here, before it is ever dereferenced or handed out:
    // count it, and take the chunk off the partial list so it stops being
    // an allocation source rather than being silently reused. O(1)
    // arithmetic, no chunk walk -- see intrusive_chunk_slot_in_range.
    //
    // The rejection is reported through the EXISTING OutOfMemory case
    // rather than a third variant case. That is this file's own stated
    // rule (see intrusive_pool_slots_per_chunk): do not make every caller
    // match on an outcome it cannot act on differently. A caller's
    // OutOfMemory path is already "I could not get a slot"; the pool
    // itself is not out of memory, and the very next insert proves it by
    // growing a fresh chunk, since quarantining left partial_head empty.
    if (intrusive_chunk_slot_in_range(pool, base, slot_address) == false) {
        header.corrupt_free_chain_count = header.corrupt_free_chain_count + 1;
        intrusive_pool_unlink_partial(pool, base);
        return IntrusivePoolInsertResult::OutOfMemory;
    }
    let slot: *IntrusiveSlot(T) = intrusive_slot_at(pool, slot_address);
    // The second half of the same check, and the cheaper-but-worse case:
    // a `next_free` corrupted to a well-formed address of a LIVE slot in
    // this same chunk passes the arithmetic above. Handing it out would
    // not fault -- it would silently alias two owners onto one slot, which
    // is worse than the wild pointer, since nothing downstream is even
    // startled by it. The invariant that catches it costs nothing and
    // needs no new state: a slot's generation is 0 for exactly as long as
    // it is free (grow zeroes it, remove zeroes it before pushing, insert
    // stamps it), so a nonzero generation on a free-chain entry is a
    // contradiction. Reading it is sound only because the range check
    // above already proved this address is one of this chunk's slots --
    // which is why the two checks are in this order and not the other.
    if (slot.generation != 0) {
        header.corrupt_free_chain_count = header.corrupt_free_chain_count + 1;
        intrusive_pool_unlink_partial(pool, base);
        return IntrusivePoolInsertResult::OutOfMemory;
    }
    // Read the link BEFORE stamping the generation: the two share this
    // slot's storage (GitHub issue #348), and stamping is exactly the
    // moment that storage stops being the chain's and becomes the
    // caller's payload.
    header.free_head = *intrusive_slot_link_at(pool, slot_address);
    let generation: usize = pool.next_generation;
    pool.next_generation = generation + 1;
    slot.generation = generation;
    // Nothing zeroes the storage here. It used to be a separate word
    // worth clearing; now it is the payload's own bytes, and writing to
    // them would be this pool inventing a value for a T it knows nothing
    // about. The contract is uniform instead: a freshly allocated
    // payload is uninitialized, write it before reading it. See "Current
    // limitations".
    pool.live_count = pool.live_count + 1;
    // Read BEFORE the increment: allocating out of a chunk that was empty
    // spends one of the held-back reserve, and after `+ 1` there is no
    // way left to tell that it had been empty (GitHub issue #354).
    if (header.live_count == 0 && pool.empty_chunk_count > 0) {
        pool.empty_chunk_count = pool.empty_chunk_count - 1;
    }
    header.live_count = header.live_count + 1;
    // Full now: it has nothing left to hand out, so it leaves the partial
    // list and rejoins on its next free.
    if (header.free_head == 0) { intrusive_pool_unlink_partial(pool, base); }
    return IntrusivePoolInsertResult::Allocated(
        intrusive_owner_new(pool, slot_address, generation));
}

// Rejects, rather than acts on, two distinguishable classes of bad handle:
// one this pool never minted (foreign_handle_count) and one whose slot is
// already free or has been reused since (double_remove_count) -- the same
// distinction kernel/lib/slotmap.tkb's slotmap_remove draws, plus the
// cross-pool case slotmap has no way to see.
// GitHub issue #371: WHICH refusal, not just that there was one.
//
// The two rejections below mean genuinely different things to a caller.
// "This address is not in any chunk I own" is a cross-pool mixup or a
// corrupted pointer -- a kernel bug. "This slot is mine but holds a
// different occupant than you think" is a use-after-free of a handle,
// which for a descriptor-shaped consumer is an ordinary race with close()
// and should become EBADF rather than trip anything.
//
// The counters stay: they are the aggregate view, and
// intrusive_pool_probe reads them. What changes is that a caller no
// longer has to diff two counters to learn which happened -- and neither
// does a test, which is how a cross-pool check in
// linux_user/tcp_pool_shape came to pass vacuously (a zero address takes
// the same counter as a foreign one).
must_use variant IntrusivePoolRemoveResult {
    Removed;
    // Not one of this pool's slots at all.
    ForeignAddress;
    // One of this pool's slots, but it no longer holds this handle's
    // occupant -- either free, or already reissued to someone else.
    StaleHandle;
}

// The two questions most callers of intrusive_pool_remove actually ask,
// so a site needing only one of them is a line rather than a three-arm
// match. Neither replaces matching the variant: a site that must ACT
// differently per reason -- the descriptor-shaped case, where a foreign
// address is a kernel bug and a stale handle is an ordinary close() race
// -- still matches it directly.
fn intrusive_remove_succeeded(result: IntrusivePoolRemoveResult) -> bool {
    match result {
        IntrusivePoolRemoveResult::Removed => { return true; }
        IntrusivePoolRemoveResult::ForeignAddress => { return false; }
        IntrusivePoolRemoveResult::StaleHandle => { return false; }
    }
}

// Both answers from ONE look, because the result is `must_use` and
// therefore affine: consuming it to ask "did it work" leaves nothing to
// ask "why not" with. Two separate accessors would force every reporting
// caller to pick one question, which is the shape this issue set out to
// remove. The phrase lives here rather than at each reporting site so two
// reports cannot describe the same refusal differently.
fn intrusive_remove_outcome(result: IntrusivePoolRemoveResult) -> (bool, *u8) {
    match result {
        IntrusivePoolRemoveResult::Removed => {
            return (true, "removed" as *u8);
        }
        IntrusivePoolRemoveResult::ForeignAddress => {
            return (false, "refused: not an address this pool owns" as *u8);
        }
        IntrusivePoolRemoveResult::StaleHandle => {
            return (false, "refused: this pool's slot, but not this occupant" as *u8);
        }
    }
}

fn intrusive_pool_remove(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                         guard: borrow IntrusivePoolGuard[pool_id],
                         owner: sink IntrusiveOwner[pool_id, allocation])
        -> IntrusivePoolRemoveResult !{unsafe} {
    let slot_address: usize = intrusive_owner_slot(owner);
    let generation: usize = intrusive_owner_generation(owner);
    if (intrusive_pool_slot_is_valid(pool, slot_address) == false) {
        pool.foreign_handle_count = pool.foreign_handle_count + 1;
        intrusive_owner_drop(owner);
        return IntrusivePoolRemoveResult::ForeignAddress;
    }
    let slot: *IntrusiveSlot(T) = intrusive_slot_at(pool, slot_address);
    if (slot.generation == 0 || slot.generation != generation) {
        pool.double_remove_count = pool.double_remove_count + 1;
        intrusive_owner_drop(owner);
        return IntrusivePoolRemoveResult::StaleHandle;
    }
    let base: usize = intrusive_chunk_base_of(pool, slot_address);
    let header: *IntrusiveChunkHeader = intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, base);
    // Clear the generation FIRST, then write the link: that order is the
    // mirror of insert's, and it is what keeps the two readings of this
    // slot's shared storage (GitHub issue #348) from ever both looking
    // valid. The payload's bytes are the chain's again from here.
    slot.generation = 0;
    *intrusive_slot_link_at(pool, slot_address) = header.free_head;
    header.free_head = slot_address;
    pool.live_count = pool.live_count - 1;
    header.live_count = header.live_count - 1;
    // Was full, now has a free slot again.
    intrusive_pool_link_partial(pool, base);
    if (header.live_count == 0 && header.corrupt_free_chain_count == 0) {
        // GitHub issue #346's hysteresis, stated once, here: hold back
        // empty chunks up to this pool's configured reserve (issue #354),
        // give every later one straight back. See IntrusivePool's own
        // comment for why the default is one.
        //
        // A quarantined chunk (issue #352) takes neither branch: it is not
        // recycled as the held-back reserve, since nothing can ever be
        // allocated from it again, and its page is NOT handed back to the
        // provider, since that would put a page with known-corrupt
        // contents back into general circulation and destroy the only
        // record that the corruption happened. It is deliberately leaked,
        // bounded by the number of corruption events -- which is zero on
        // any system behaving correctly.
        if (pool.empty_chunk_count < pool.retained_chunks) {
            pool.empty_chunk_count = pool.empty_chunk_count + 1;
        } else {
            intrusive_pool_release_chunk(pool, base);
        }
    }
    intrusive_owner_drop(owner);
    return IntrusivePoolRemoveResult::Removed;
}

// The capability kernel/lib/slotmap.tkb's slotmap_matches and
// growable_pool_matches both provide, over a raw (address, generation)
// pair rather than an (index, generation) one: does this slot still hold
// this exact occupant? No ownership proof required, and safe to call on an
// address of unknown provenance.
fn intrusive_pool_matches(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                          slot_address: usize, generation: usize)
        -> bool !{unsafe} {
    if (generation == 0) { return false; }
    if (intrusive_pool_slot_is_valid(pool, slot_address) == false) { return false; }
    return intrusive_slot_at(pool, slot_address).generation == generation;
}

// Fast path, no re-validation: see this file's "Current limitations".
// Field access through the result needs no explicit deref
// (`intrusive_pool_ref(&pool, owner).field = 1;`); a whole-T read or write
// does -- the same shape kernel/lib/freelist.tkb's freelist_ref has.
fn intrusive_pool_ref(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                      owner: borrow IntrusiveOwner[pool_id, allocation]) -> *T !{unsafe} {
    return intrusive_pool_payload_at(pool, intrusive_owner_slot(owner));
}

// PRIVATE since GitHub issue #348's second half. This is the raw form --
// an address in, a `*T` out, no proof of anything -- and once the payload
// and the free-chain link share storage, calling it on a FREE slot reads
// a chain pointer as a T. Callers outside this file go through
// intrusive_pool_probe_slot instead, which is the same work with the
// result they have to look at.
private fn intrusive_pool_payload_at(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                                     slot_address: usize) -> *T !{unsafe} {
    return unsafe {
        (slot_address + offsetof(IntrusiveSlot(T), storage)) as *T
    };
}

// -- Proving a slot is live --------------------------------------------
// GitHub issue #348: with the payload and the free-chain link sharing one
// storage word, "is this slot live?" stopped being advisory. Reading a
// free slot's payload used to give a previous occupant's remains --
// wrong, but inert. It now gives a pointer reinterpreted as a T.
//
// The old contract was a comment: callers of intrusive_pool_payload_at
// filter on intrusive_pool_slot_generation() != 0 first. Nothing checked
// that they did. IntrusiveSlotView turns it into an obligation the
// compiler tracks -- the payload accessor takes a view, the only way to
// get one is intrusive_pool_probe_slot, and the only way probe_slot mints
// one is on the branch where it has just read a nonzero generation.
//
// The private fields are what make that airtight: a struct with private
// fields cannot be literal-constructed outside its declaring file, so no
// other file can forge a view for an address of its choosing. `pool_id`
// carries the same brand IntrusiveOwner does (issue #345), so a view
// minted by one pool cannot be spent on another.
//
// It is a linear STRUCT rather than an erased `linear view`, and that is
// forced rather than chosen. An erased view carries its subject as a
// static index, and opening the existential a variant payload needs mints
// a FRESH identity -- the compiler rejects using the resulting permission
// with the address it came from ("static value mismatch: s vs slot").
// examples/tcp_conn_view never hits that because nothing in it uses the
// concrete connection; an allocator's whole purpose is to touch memory at
// the address, so it does. Carrying the address as a runtime field
// sidesteps the comparison entirely. The cost is 16 stack bytes per probe
// in flight, never anything per slot.
private linear struct IntrusiveSlotView[pool_id: addr] {
    private slot: usize;
    private generation: usize;
}

// Two cases, not three. "Not ours" and "free" are both "there is no
// payload here" to every caller a scan has, and this file's own rule --
// stated at intrusive_pool_slots_per_chunk and again at insert's
// corruption path -- is not to make callers match on outcomes they
// cannot act on differently.
must_use variant IntrusiveSlotProbe[pool_id: addr] {
    NoPayload;
    Live(IntrusiveSlotView[pool_id]);
}

// The one entry point that mints a view, and the replacement for
// "intrusive_pool_slot_generation() != 0, then intrusive_pool_payload_at".
// Safe on an address of unknown provenance: validation happens before the
// generation is read, exactly as intrusive_pool_slot_generation did.
// Costs the caller nothing extra -- it is the same validate-and-read that
// filter already performed, with the result in a form that cannot be
// skipped.
fn intrusive_pool_probe_slot(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                             slot_address: usize) -> IntrusiveSlotProbe[pool_id] !{unsafe} {
    if (intrusive_pool_slot_is_valid(pool, slot_address) == false) {
        return IntrusiveSlotProbe::NoPayload;
    }
    let generation: usize = intrusive_slot_at(pool, slot_address).generation;
    if (generation == 0) {
        return IntrusiveSlotProbe::NoPayload;
    }
    let mut minted: IntrusiveSlotView[pool_id] = { slot_address, generation };
    return IntrusiveSlotProbe::Live(minted);
}

fn intrusive_view_slot(slot_view: borrow IntrusiveSlotView[pool_id]) -> usize {
    return slot_view.slot;
}

// The occupant stamp the view was minted against -- what a caller needs
// to rebuild an owner for a slot it found by scanning.
fn intrusive_view_generation(slot_view: borrow IntrusiveSlotView[pool_id]) -> usize {
    return slot_view.generation;
}

// Discharges the obligation for a caller that looked and moved on.
fn intrusive_view_drop(slot_view: sink IntrusiveSlotView[pool_id]) {}

// The gated payload accessor. `sink` rather than `borrow` on purpose: the
// view is a snapshot of a fact that a later insert or remove can undo, so
// spending it here keeps a `*T` from being justified by a proof the
// caller kept holding after it went stale.
fn intrusive_pool_payload_of(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                             slot_view: sink IntrusiveSlotView[pool_id]) -> *T !{unsafe} {
    return intrusive_pool_payload_at(pool, intrusive_view_slot(slot_view));
}

// -- Iteration ---------------------------------------------------------
// A consumer like kernel/net/tcp.tkb does not only allocate and free: it
// scans its pool looking for the connection matching a received segment.
// With a fixed `[T; N]` that is `for i in 0..<N`; here it is a cursor walk
// over the chunk list. Both intrusive_pool_first_slot and
// intrusive_pool_next_slot return 0 for "no more", and visit FREE slots
// too -- so a scan matches on intrusive_pool_probe_slot and reads the
// payload only through the view its Live case carries. That match IS the
// filter this section used to describe as a convention.

fn intrusive_pool_first_slot(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id) -> usize !{unsafe} {
    if (pool.chunk_head == 0) { return 0; }
    return pool.chunk_head;
}

fn intrusive_pool_next_slot(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                            slot_address: usize) -> usize !{unsafe} {
    if (intrusive_pool_slot_is_valid(pool, slot_address) == false) { return 0; }
    let base: usize = intrusive_chunk_base_of(pool, slot_address);
    let header: *IntrusiveChunkHeader = intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, base);
    let index: usize =
        (slot_address - base) / sizeof(IntrusiveSlot(T));
    if (index + 1 < intrusive_pool_slots_per_chunk(pool)) {
        return slot_address + sizeof(IntrusiveSlot(T));
    }
    if (header.next_chunk == 0) { return 0; }
    return header.next_chunk;
}

// 0 means "this slot is free". Safe on an address of unknown provenance.
//
// PRIVATE since issue #348's second half: handing out a bare liveness
// integer is what let a caller decide, on its own, that reading the
// payload was fine. intrusive_pool_probe_slot returns the same
// observation in a form that carries the permission with it. Kept for
// this file's own use, where the surrounding code is the proof.
private fn intrusive_pool_slot_generation(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                                          slot_address: usize) -> usize !{unsafe} {
    if (intrusive_pool_slot_is_valid(pool, slot_address) == false) { return 0; }
    return intrusive_slot_at(pool, slot_address).generation;
}

// -- Observers ---------------------------------------------------------

fn intrusive_pool_live_count(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id) -> usize {
    return pool.live_count;
}

fn intrusive_pool_chunk_count(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id) -> usize {
    return pool.chunk_count;
}

fn intrusive_pool_double_remove_count(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id) -> usize {
    return pool.double_remove_count;
}

fn intrusive_pool_foreign_handle_count(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id) -> usize {
    return pool.foreign_handle_count;
}

// GitHub issue #352: how many times a popped free-chain entry failed the
// alloc-path range check -- a corrupted `next_free` word, or a corrupted
// chunk `free_head`, caught before its address was ever handed out.
//
// Summed over the chunk list (O(chunks)) rather than read from a pool
// field, because the count lives in the chunk headers' already-reserved
// room: see IntrusiveChunkHeader's own comment for why that is the free
// place to put it. This is a diagnostic observer, never an allocation
// path, so the walk costs nothing that matters -- and it is now the only
// O(chunks) walk left in the file, since issue #349 made validation
// O(1).
fn intrusive_pool_corrupt_free_chain_count(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id)
        -> usize !{unsafe} {
    let mut total: usize = 0;
    let mut cursor: usize = pool.chunk_head;
    let mut steps: usize = 0;
    while (cursor != 0 && intrusive_pool_owns_chunk(pool, cursor) &&
           steps <= pool.chunk_count) {
        // Same defensiveness as intrusive_pool_probe's walk, and for the
        // same reason: this observer is read when something has already
        // gone wrong, so it must not assume the list survived.
        steps = steps + 1;
        total = total + intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, cursor).corrupt_free_chain_count;
        cursor = intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, cursor).next_chunk;
    }
    return total;
}

// How many chunks this pool has given back to the page provider, for a
// caller (or a test) that wants to see reclaim actually happening rather
// than infer it from the provider's own live count.
fn intrusive_pool_released_chunk_count(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id)
        -> usize {
    return pool.released_chunk_count;
}

// -- Invariant probe ---------------------------------------------------
// GitHub issue #355. The counters above say how much; this says whether
// what they say is still coherent. Allocator bugs surface arbitrarily far
// from their cause, and the first question is always "what does the pool
// look like right now" -- a question five scalars cannot answer.
//
// What it checks is deliberately narrow, because most of what a
// `slabinfo` exists to find is unreachable here by construction: leaks
// and double frees are linearity violations, and cross-pool frees are
// type errors. What is NOT ruled out by construction is exactly the list
// and chain surgery reclaim does (issue #346), so that is what this
// verifies:
//
//   per chunk  every free-chain entry is a well-formed slot of THIS chunk
//   per chunk  the chain terminates -- a cycle would otherwise hang any
//              walk of it, including this one, which is why the walk is
//              bounded rather than trusting termination
//   per chunk  chain length + live_count == slots per chunk
//   per chunk  partial-list membership matches "has a free slot"
//   per chunk  live_count does not exceed the chunk
//   per pool   the chunk-list length and the live total agree with
//              chunk_count and live_count
//
// The first two are what issue #361 could not reach by deriving. The
// slot BOUNDS became underivable-corruption-proof by not being stored;
// `free_head` and the links are genuinely dynamic, so the only defence
// available is to check them, and this is where that happens.
//
// A QUARANTINED chunk (issue #352) is exempted from the chain and
// partial-list checks, and that is not a loophole: quarantine means the
// pool has already ruled the chain untrustworthy, refused to allocate
// from it, and counted the event. Demanding coherence from it here would
// report the corruption the pool already caught and handled as a fresh
// failure.
//
// Cost is O(all slots) -- the free chains are walked entry by entry. A
// diagnostic, never a hot path; call it between operations, not inside
// them.
fn intrusive_pool_probe(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id) -> bool !{unsafe} {
    let slots: usize = intrusive_pool_slots_per_chunk(pool);
    let mut ok: bool = true;
    let mut chunks_seen: usize = 0;
    let mut live_seen: usize = 0;
    let mut cursor: usize = pool.chunk_head;
    let mut steps: usize = 0;
    while (cursor != 0) {
        // Corruption is the case this exists FOR, so the walk cannot
        // assume the list is well-formed. Each link is checked before it
        // is followed -- a link pointing outside this pool ends the walk
        // rather than steering a dereference -- and the whole walk is
        // bounded, so a cycle is reported instead of hanging. Without
        // both, a probe run on a damaged pool crashes, which is exactly
        // when someone is running it.
        if (intrusive_pool_owns_chunk(pool, cursor) == false) {
            ok = false;
            cursor = 0;
        } else if (steps > pool.chunk_count) {
            ok = false;
            cursor = 0;
        } else {
        steps = steps + 1;
        let header: *IntrusiveChunkHeader = intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, cursor);
        chunks_seen = chunks_seen + 1;
        live_seen = live_seen + header.live_count;
        if (header.live_count > slots) { ok = false; }
        let quarantined: bool = header.corrupt_free_chain_count != 0;
        let chain_len: usize = intrusive_chunk_free_chain_length(pool, cursor);
        if (quarantined == false) {
            // A chain that did not terminate cleanly reports slots + 1,
            // which cannot equal slots - live_count for any live_count.
            if (chain_len + header.live_count != slots) { ok = false; }
            let has_free: bool = header.free_head != 0;
            let listed: bool = header.on_partial != 0;
            if (has_free && listed == false) { ok = false; }
            if (has_free == false && listed) { ok = false; }
        }
        cursor = header.next_chunk;
        }
    }
    if (chunks_seen != pool.chunk_count) { ok = false; }
    if (live_seen != pool.live_count) { ok = false; }
    return ok;
}

// -- Dump surface ------------------------------------------------------
// The facts a human-readable dump wants, without this file taking on an
// output dependency -- growable_pool keeps itself free of
// one too, and a pool primitive that can only be inspected on a target
// with a working console is a worse primitive. The caller formats.
//
// This is also the gating issue #355 asks for: these are ordinary
// functions nobody has to call, so an unused dump costs exactly nothing.

fn intrusive_pool_first_chunk(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id) -> usize {
    return pool.chunk_head;
}

fn intrusive_pool_next_chunk(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                             base: usize) -> usize !{unsafe} {
    if (intrusive_pool_owns_chunk(pool, base) == false) { return 0; }
    return intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, base).next_chunk;
}

fn intrusive_chunk_live_count(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                              base: usize) -> usize !{unsafe} {
    if (intrusive_pool_owns_chunk(pool, base) == false) { return 0; }
    return intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, base).live_count;
}

fn intrusive_chunk_on_partial(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                              base: usize) -> bool !{unsafe} {
    if (intrusive_pool_owns_chunk(pool, base) == false) { return false; }
    return intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, base).on_partial != 0;
}

fn intrusive_chunk_is_quarantined(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                                  base: usize) -> bool !{unsafe} {
    if (intrusive_pool_owns_chunk(pool, base) == false) { return false; }
    return intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, base).corrupt_free_chain_count != 0;
}

// How many slots this chunk's free chain actually holds. Bounded by the
// slot count and reporting `slots + 1` on overrun, so a corrupted chain
// -- including one that cycles -- terminates the walk and is visible as
// an impossible length rather than hanging the caller. Entries that are
// not well-formed slots of this chunk stop the walk at that point, which
// is what makes a truncated chain show up as a length mismatch.
fn intrusive_chunk_free_chain_length(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                                     base: usize) -> usize !{unsafe} {
    if (intrusive_pool_owns_chunk(pool, base) == false) { return 0; }
    let slots: usize = intrusive_pool_slots_per_chunk(pool);
    let mut entry: usize = intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, base).free_head;
    let mut length: usize = 0;
    let mut walking: bool = true;
    while (walking) {
        if (entry == 0) {
            walking = false;
        } else if (length >= slots) {
            length = slots + 1;
            walking = false;
        } else if (intrusive_chunk_slot_in_range(pool, base, entry) == false) {
            walking = false;
        } else {
            length = length + 1;
            entry = *intrusive_slot_link_at(pool, entry);
        }
    }
    return length;
}

// -- Reclaim on demand -------------------------------------------------
// GitHub issue #354. Reclaim was reactive only: a page went back when a
// chunk happened to empty through an ordinary free, and the reserve was
// unreachable. Under memory pressure every pool in the system would hoard
// its reserve at exactly the moment memory was scarce, with no way to ask
// for it back.
//
// This is that ask. It returns every empty chunk INCLUDING the retained
// reserve, and resets the reserve count so the pool starts hoarding again
// from zero rather than believing it still holds pages it gave away.
// Returns how many pages went back, which is what a caller under pressure
// wants to know.
//
// Quarantined chunks stay, for the reason they always stay (issue #352):
// handing a page with known-corrupt contents back into general
// circulation trades a bounded leak for an unbounded problem.
//
// The walk captures `next` before releasing, because releasing unlinks --
// and it is the same defensive walk intrusive_pool_probe uses, for the
// same reason: a pool under memory pressure is not obviously a pool in
// good health.
//
// NO GLOBAL SWEEP, and that is a decision rather than an omission. A
// "reclaim from every pool" entry point needs a registry of live pools,
// and three things make that the wrong shape here:
//
//   - Pools are typed. A registry would be heterogeneous, so calling back
//     into each pool needs type erasure plus dispatch through a function
//     pointer -- machinery this codebase has consistently declined.
//   - The brand is the problem, not an obstacle to route around. Every
//     entry point takes `&mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id`, a COMPILE-TIME
//     identity; an address stored in a registry and reconstituted later
//     cannot carry it, so a sweep would have to give up exactly the
//     guarantee issue #345 added.
//   - The registry is itself a growable collection, so its natural
//     storage is this primitive -- the same regress issue #349's
//     header-pool bootstrap ran into.
//
// The shape that works instead needs nothing new: a kernel's pools are a
// fixed, statically known set, so a memory-pressure path calls
// intrusive_pool_reclaim on each by name. That is a list at the call
// site, checked by the compiler, with every brand intact -- strictly
// better than a runtime registry, and it is why this file stops here.
fn intrusive_pool_reclaim(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id,
                          guard: borrow IntrusivePoolGuard[pool_id])
        -> usize !{unsafe} {
    let released_before: usize = pool.released_chunk_count;
    let mut cursor: usize = pool.chunk_head;
    let mut steps: usize = 0;
    while (cursor != 0 && intrusive_pool_owns_chunk(pool, cursor) &&
           steps <= pool.chunk_count) {
        steps = steps + 1;
        let header: *IntrusiveChunkHeader = intrusive_chunk_header_at(pool, cursor);
        let next: usize = header.next_chunk;
        if (header.live_count == 0 && header.corrupt_free_chain_count == 0) {
            intrusive_pool_release_chunk(pool, cursor);
        }
        cursor = next;
    }
    pool.empty_chunk_count = 0;
    return pool.released_chunk_count - released_before;
}

// What this pool is currently allowed to hold empty, and what it actually
// holds -- the pair a caller deciding whether reclaim is worth calling
// wants, without walking anything.
fn intrusive_pool_retained_chunks(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id)
        -> usize {
    return pool.retained_chunks;
}

fn intrusive_pool_empty_chunk_count(T: type, pool: &mut IntrusivePool(T) @ pool_id)
        -> usize {
    return pool.empty_chunk_count;
}

// GitHub issue #257: the boot-time probe that runs this file against the
// REAL page provider, mirroring growable_pool.tkb's own growable_pool_probe.
//
// linux_user/intrusive_pool exercises far more of this file than this
// does, and it does it against a stand-in provider with a five-page
// supply. What it structurally cannot reach is the thing this probe is
// for: whether the nine symbols issue #364 left unnamed actually bind to
// kernel/mm/page.tkb, whether a chunk's header really lands in that
// provider's per-page metadata, and whether a slot address computed from
// a real physical page is one the MMU has mapped. Those are exactly the
// facts that a fake provider agrees with regardless of the truth.
//
// So this checks the seam, not the algorithm: one real page per chunk,
// payload that survives a write and a read back through a real mapping,
// a stale handle refused after the slot is recycled, the invariant probe
// clean, and the pages given back.
//
// Reports a page-count DELTA rather than an absolute, and never calls
// page_allocator_reclaim_all() -- other probes run earlier in boot and
// hold pages of their own, exactly as growable_pool_probe's own comment
// records.
fn intrusive_pool_boot_probe() -> bool !{unsafe} {
    let mut pool: IntrusivePool(usize);
    intrusive_pool_init(&pool);
    let live_before: usize = page_allocator_live_count();

    let g0 = intrusive_pool_lock(&pool);
    let first = intrusive_pool_insert(&pool, g0);
    intrusive_pool_unlock(&pool, g0);
    let a0 = match first {
        IntrusivePoolInsertResult::OutOfMemory => { return false; }
        IntrusivePoolInsertResult::Allocated(o) => { o }
    };
    // One chunk is one RUN, whose page count this pool derives from T
    // (issue #350) -- a `usize` payload takes the smallest order, one
    // page -- and the provider is the real one.
    if (page_allocator_live_count() !=
            live_before + intrusive_pool_chunk_pages(&pool) ||
        intrusive_pool_chunk_count(&pool) != 1) {
        let gx = intrusive_pool_lock(&pool);
        // The probe is already failing; this is only tidying up after
        // itself, so the answer is looked at and discarded deliberately
        // rather than left unasked.
        let tidy: bool =
            intrusive_remove_succeeded(intrusive_pool_remove(&pool, gx, a0));
        intrusive_pool_unlock(&pool, gx);
        if (tidy == false) { return false; }
        return false;
    }

    // The payload is reached through a pointer into a real physical page
    // this kernel has mapped; a write and a read back is the cheapest
    // thing that fails loudly if it is not.
    let p0: *usize = intrusive_pool_ref(&pool, a0);
    *p0 = 0xFEEDFACE;
    let slot0: usize = intrusive_owner_slot(a0);
    let generation0: usize = intrusive_owner_generation(a0);
    let p0_again: *usize = intrusive_pool_ref(&pool, a0);
    let stored_ok: bool = *p0_again == 0xFEEDFACE;

    let g1 = intrusive_pool_lock(&pool);
    let removed_ok: bool =
        intrusive_remove_succeeded(intrusive_pool_remove(&pool, g1, a0));
    intrusive_pool_unlock(&pool, g1);

    // The freed slot comes back (the chunk free list is LIFO), and the
    // handle that named its previous occupant must not.
    let g2 = intrusive_pool_lock(&pool);
    let second = intrusive_pool_insert(&pool, g2);
    intrusive_pool_unlock(&pool, g2);
    let a1 = match second {
        IntrusivePoolInsertResult::OutOfMemory => { return false; }
        IntrusivePoolInsertResult::Allocated(o) => { o }
    };
    let recycled: bool = intrusive_owner_slot(a1) == slot0;
    let stale_refused: bool =
        intrusive_pool_matches(&pool, slot0, generation0) == false;
    let removes_before: usize = intrusive_pool_double_remove_count(&pool);
    let stale = intrusive_owner_new(&pool, slot0, generation0);
    let g3 = intrusive_pool_lock(&pool);
    let stale_result = intrusive_pool_remove(&pool, g3, stale);
    intrusive_pool_unlock(&pool, g3);
    // GitHub issue #371: the REASON, not a counter delta. A use-after-free
    // of a handle and a pointer from another pool both moved a counter
    // before this; only one of them is this case, and only the returned
    // reason says so.
    let mut stale_named: bool = false;
    match stale_result {
        IntrusivePoolRemoveResult::Removed => {}
        IntrusivePoolRemoveResult::ForeignAddress => {}
        IntrusivePoolRemoveResult::StaleHandle => { stale_named = true; }
    }
    let stale_counted: bool = stale_named &&
        intrusive_pool_double_remove_count(&pool) == removes_before + 1 &&
        intrusive_pool_live_count(&pool) == 1;

    let invariants_ok: bool = intrusive_pool_probe(&pool);

    let g4 = intrusive_pool_lock(&pool);
    let removed_a1_ok: bool =
        intrusive_remove_succeeded(intrusive_pool_remove(&pool, g4, a1));
    intrusive_pool_unlock(&pool, g4);
    // The default reserve holds the emptied chunk, so ask for it back
    // explicitly -- otherwise this probe leaves a page behind for the
    // rest of boot, which is the accounting other probes read.
    let g5 = intrusive_pool_lock(&pool);
    let released: usize = intrusive_pool_reclaim(&pool, g5);
    intrusive_pool_unlock(&pool, g5);
    let pages_returned: bool =
        released == 1 && page_allocator_live_count() == live_before;

    return stored_ok && removed_ok && removed_a1_ok && recycled &&
           stale_refused && stale_counted && invariants_ok && pages_returned;
}
